\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Symmetry of a marginal and restriction of a precision}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The sector laws use an ordinary orthogonal finite-group action on
a real field space and a strictly positive precision. These
hypotheses allow independent Gaussian factors and equivariant
elimination. They do not make every stable retained subspace a
reducing subspace for the precision. A stable vertex set says that
its coordinate projection commutes with the group action;
[cycle decimation](fdip-0057) shows that it need not commute with
#{Q}. The resulting Schur precision can therefore differ from the
compression #{Q_{RR}}. Commuting with the group action and commuting
with #{Q} are distinct conditions.}

\p{Three other representation-theoretic questions need additional
data. [Projective representations and twisted group algebras](fgap-001S)
record a multiplier rather than an ordinary group action;
[the projective obstruction](fgap-001T) explains its role.
[Restriction to a normal subgroup](fgap-001O) and
[Clifford's homogeneity theorem](fgap-001Q) concern constituents
under a specified subgroup inclusion; this is Clifford theory of
group representations, distinct from Clifford algebras.
In [positive characteristic](fdip-000N), the
averaging denominator or Gaussian measure used here may be absent.
None of these is supplied by real isotypic factorization alone.}
